## 5. Strict authentication requirements

This is one of the most important parts of the entire project.

### Public visitor experience

The public website must have NO:
- Login button.
- Sign-in button.
- Register button.
- Create account button.
- User profile system.
- Public user dashboard.
- User password reset flow.
- Public authentication page.
- Public session-based member area.

Do not create `/login`, `/register`, `/signup`, `/signin`, or similar public account pages.

Do not put any account icon in the public navigation that leads to authentication.

Do not add user authentication just because the framework or starter template provides it by default.

Visitors should be able to browse all public pages without an account.

### Private team administration

Create exactly one intended team login entry point:

`https://subdomain.domain/admin/login`

Treat this as a deployment URL pattern. Replace `subdomain.domain` with the actual domain when configuring production.

The login page must contain:
- Username field.
- Password field.
- Sign In button.
- Clear validation and authentication error messages.
- A professional dark-themed design consistent with Black Cloud.
- No public registration option.
- No public password-reset or account-creation workflow.

Use the following initial administrator identity:

Username: `DevAdam`

The initial administrator password is the password supplied by the studio owner. Configure it through a secure deployment secret named `ADMIN_PASSWORD`, not by hardcoding the plaintext password in source files.

Provide clear deployment instructions explaining how to set this initial secret securely.

Never expose the administrator password in client-side JavaScript, HTML, frontend environment variables, public API responses, Git repositories, logs, or generated documentation.

The login form must authenticate against the server-side administrator record or the configured authentication provider. Do not merely compare credentials in the browser.

Store passwords using the secure password-handling mechanism of the selected authentication provider. If using a custom authentication system, use a strong password hash such as Argon2id.

The administrator username must be unique, and public account registration must be disabled.

### Admin URL and authorization

The only intended team login route is `/admin/login`.

The protected administration interface should live under `/admin`, with `/admin` redirecting unauthenticated visitors to `/admin/login`.

For example:
- `/admin/login` — team login.
- `/admin` — dashboard.
- `/admin/games` — game management.
- `/admin/news` — news management.
- `/admin/tools` — cybersecurity tool catalog management.
- `/admin/ctf` — CTF and research management.
- `/admin/settings` — studio settings.

These are private administration routes, not public user accounts.

A hidden URL alone is not security. Protect every admin API endpoint and every sensitive database operation with server-side authentication and authorization checks.

Unauthenticated requests must receive an appropriate 401 response. Authenticated users without the required permissions must receive a 403 response.

Use secure session cookies with HttpOnly, Secure in production, and an appropriate SameSite setting. Implement CSRF protection where applicable, login rate limiting, secure logout, session expiration, and safe session rotation after authentication.

Do not expose admin credentials or protected data to the public client.

If the administrator has not been configured securely, display a deployment error and refuse to enable production administration rather than falling back to default credentials.

